Exercise 8.13 asks to prove that the universal domain \(U\) is isomorphic to the domain of proper filters of the free Boolean algebra on countably many generators, identified with the Lindenbaum algebra of propositional calculus, and to connect that representation with the nonempty open subsets of Cantor space \(2^{\mathbb{N}}\). `example_813_a`, which is `exercise813a`, states only the domain isomorphism \(U\cong^{\mathrm{D}} V\), obtained by applying `toDomainIso` to `effectiveIso812_UV`. `V_mem_iff_generatedBy` proves that the neighbourhoods of \(V\) are the nonempty sets generated by the bit-sets `generator`, using `generatedBy_levelSet` one way and `generatedBy_generator_imp` the other, and `generators_independent` proves that every finite Boolean combination of generators and complements is nonempty by taking the bitmask of the coordinates assigned true. `Lindenbaum` is the quotient of `Formula` by `SemanticEquiv`; `Lindenbaumu2etoSet_injective` and `Lindenbaumu2erange_toSet` show that `toSet` is a bijection onto the algebra generated by `generator`, the first by reducing equal images to semantic equivalence and the second by induction on classes and on formulas. Those stems leave the proper-filter reading of `V.Element` unstated, and the gloss of `exercise813a` states only \(U\cong V\). `example_813_b` is `entails_iff_evalSet_subset`, entailment of formulas as inclusion of evaluation sets, proved by the bit bridge `mem_evalSet_iff` one way and by `exists_bitsOf_agree` with `evalV_eq_of_agree` the other. `example_813_c` is `generatedBy_of_mem_dualFilter`: a member of the dual of a family of generated sets is generated, because its complement lies in the family and the complement of a generated set is generated. On the topological side, `isClopen_iff_generatedBy_genPoint` identifies the clopens of Cantor space with `GeneratedBy genPoint`, the substantive direction extracting a finite subcover of boxes from compactness and citing `generatedBy_genPoint_biUnion`, and `isOpen_iff_exists_iUnion_generatedBy` writes an open set as the union of the generated sets it contains by the product-box characterization. `elementOfOpen` sends a set \(O\) of Cantor space with \(O\neq\mathrm{univ}\) to the `V.Element` of those subsets of \(\mathbb{N}\) that `Corresponds` relates to a member of `dualFilter (idealOf O)`; nonemptiness of the witness uses `univ_not_mem_idealOf`, `not_empty_mem_dualFilter`, and `Corresponds.nonempty_iff`, the master uses `empty_mem_idealOf`, intersection uses `union_mem_idealOf` and `Corresponds.inter`, and the upward step uses a formula for the larger \(V\)-set together with `up_mem_dualFilter`. The hypothesis is inequality with the whole space, so openness and nonemptiness of \(O\) are not assumed, and no stem asserts that this map is bijective onto the elements of \(V\). `toSet'_mk` describes the Lindenbaum map as landing in Cantor space; the equation is `Lindenbaum.toSet' ⟦φ⟧ = evalSet' φ`, a set of points of that space. **Theorem:** partial — \(U\cong V\) and the bijection of the Lindenbaum algebra onto the generated algebra are proved, and the proper-filter isomorphism together with a correspondence for the nonempty opens remain outside the numbered statements. **Proof:** partial — the bit-algebra, entailment, clopen-compactness, and `elementOfOpen` arguments match their stems, and `example_813_c` proves only dual generation.
